## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to formalize DP for partitioning into constrained blocks and compare top-down vs bottom-up implementations with proper modulo handling.

## Key Concepts
- Top-down memoization with pruning
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Positive number optimization
- Modulo arithmetic and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function countPartitions(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (i === n) return 1;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];

    let sum = 0, ways = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      sum += nums[j];
      if (sum > target) break;
      if (sum === target) {
        ways = (ways + dfs(j + 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    return memo[i] = ways;
  }

  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulation sketch
function countPartitionsBottomUp(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n && sum + nums[j] <= target; j++) {
      sum += nums[j];
      if (sum === target) {
        dp[j+1] = (dp[j+1] + dp[i]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Empty input: return 0
- Large target: handle with modulo and pruning
- Single element equal to target: return 1
- No valid partition: return 0

## Correctness Justification
Top-down explores all valid partitions by recursively trying all possible block endings. Memoization avoids recomputation. Bottom-up builds from base case, accumulating ways to reach each position. Both respect positive numbers enabling early termination when sum exceeds target.

## Concept Overview
An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache efficiently manages memory by evicting the least accessed items when capacity is reached. This implementation leverages a HashMap for fast key lookups and a DoublyLinkedList for maintaining access order.

## Implementation Details
- **HashMap**: Maps keys to Node references, enabling O(1) lookup operations
- **DoublyLinkedList**: Maintains access order with head representing most recently used items and tail representing least recently used items
- **Thread Safety**: ReentrantReadWriteLock provides optimal concurrency - multiple threads can read simultaneously while writes are mutually exclusive
- **Eviction Policy**: When capacity is exceeded, the tail node (least recently used) is removed
- **Operations**: Both get() and put() operations maintain O(1) time complexity

## Code Example
```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> cache;
    private final DoublyLinkedList<K, V> order;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock;
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new HashMap<>();
        this.order = new DoublyLinkedList<>();
        this.lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> node = cache.get(key);
            if (node != null) {
                // Upgrade to write lock for reordering
                lock.readLock().unlock();
                lock.writeLock().lock();
                order.moveToHead(node);
                return node.value;
            }
            return null;
        } finally {
            if (lock.isWriteLocked()) {
                lock.writeLock().unlock();
            } else {
                lock.readLock().unlock();
            }
        }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> existing = cache.get(key);
            if (existing != null) {
                existing.value = value;
                order.moveToHead(existing);
                return;
            }

            Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
            cache.put(key, newNode);
            order.addToHead(newNode);

            if (cache.size() > capacity) {
                Node<K, V> tail = order.removeTail();
                cache.remove(tail.key);
            }
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}```

